// Blocks ways that OSM's conditional tags close to pedestrians on the day the
// graph was built. Applied to foot, hike, stroller and easyhike.
//
// Separate from foot_carriageway.json on purpose, even though the two go on
// exactly the same four profiles. This is an access rule -- it takes a way out of
// the graph for everyone -- while that one only says which of the ways you may
// use are nicer to walk on. Merging them would put a
// multiply_by 0 in the middle of a list of preferences, which is the kind of
// thing that gets tuned by accident later. It also keeps the three vehicles
// symmetrical: car_temporal.json and bike_temporal.json are their own files too.
//
// car_temporal.json carries the full explanation of the mechanism: the date is
// resolved at import against date_range_parser_day, dates and seasons only, and
// the permissive direction is read too, so a way tagged only
// `foot:conditional=yes @ (May-Oct)` comes out NO in January.
//
// The one judgement specific to this file: upstream suggests a hint rather than a
// block for foot, on the grounds that pedestrian conditionals are often minor. We
// block anyway, for consistency with the other two and because the alternative
// needs a path detail the web app would have to render -- and 11.0 has no
// `access_conditional` detail to render.
{
  "priority": [
    { "if": "foot_temporal_access == NO", "multiply_by": "0" }
  ]
}
